1
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:04,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, something else is your situation on the general level.

2
00:00:04,334 --> 00:00:07,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see what I mean?

3
00:00:09,636 --> 00:00:13,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You are mindful that you are here in the cave, sitting on the floor, while you're talking the dhamma.

4
00:00:14,497 --> 00:00:20,159
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you can have a thought of you, sitting in the cave, on the floor, talking dhamma.

5
00:00:20,519 --> 00:00:22,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And that thought is particular for you now.

6
00:00:24,901 --> 00:00:28,902
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're sitting in the cave, on the floor, talking about the dhamma, which remains general.

7
00:00:29,622 --> 00:00:31,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So it can be the same thought,

8
00:00:32,215 --> 00:00:36,417
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see it twicely or doubly, simultaneously.

9
00:00:36,437 --> 00:00:43,361
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see the same thought on the particular level and you can see it on the same, like, same thought on a more general level.

10
00:00:45,722 --> 00:00:48,144
[SPEAKER_00]: That's immediacy and reflection approach.

11
00:00:49,084 --> 00:00:52,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's the same structure, same principle, same generality.

12
00:00:57,734 --> 00:01:03,298
[SPEAKER_00]: What you should not do is confuse, then, the particular things for general and think that they are general.

13
00:01:04,179 --> 00:01:06,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they are particular.

14
00:01:06,621 --> 00:01:11,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And it means if you see them as general, then their general became particular and now something else is general.

15
00:01:12,945 --> 00:01:13,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's the same thing.

16
00:01:14,086 --> 00:01:17,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you lose the sight of the general, you lose the sight of the mindfulness.

17
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:19,891
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite difficult, I think.

18
00:01:21,712 --> 00:01:22,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very unnatural.

19
00:01:23,913 --> 00:01:25,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Because natural means ignorant.

20
00:01:27,335 --> 00:01:32,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Natural means going with the grain, as the Buddha referred to it.

21
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:37,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But the practice of dhamma goes against the grain, against the way of thinking.

22
00:01:38,459 --> 00:01:41,380
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean you kind of go mad and do crazy things.

23
00:01:41,861 --> 00:01:44,642
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means you start thinking in the opposite direction.

24
00:01:46,162 --> 00:01:48,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the opposite direction.

25
00:01:48,463 --> 00:01:51,224
[SPEAKER_00]: The most common thing is me, my cup, I want to drink the water.

26
00:01:51,464 --> 00:01:53,265
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you start thinking indirectly.

27
00:01:54,424 --> 00:01:59,268
[SPEAKER_00]: I can drink this water only because my situation allows it as a possibility.

28
00:02:00,229 --> 00:02:01,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Water to be drank.

29
00:02:04,312 --> 00:02:06,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So you start bypassing your sense of self.

30
00:02:06,634 --> 00:02:08,295
[SPEAKER_00]: You start making it redundant.

31
00:02:09,416 --> 00:02:15,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whether I want to drink water or not, the water is possible to be drank because of the situation.

32
00:02:16,873 --> 00:02:18,073
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you can attend it.

33
00:02:18,093 --> 00:02:24,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, my sense of self that comes with this intention, I want to drink the water, depends upon that intention, I want to drink the water.

34
00:02:25,296 --> 00:02:27,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is given by this situation that it's not in my control.

35
00:02:28,277 --> 00:02:32,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, my sense of self is something I can't control either.

36
00:02:34,279 --> 00:02:35,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it's not my sense of self.

37
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:43,281
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to define your situation.

38
00:02:44,002 --> 00:02:45,302
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just know situation.

39
00:02:46,158 --> 00:02:47,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because you're already in.

40
00:02:47,298 --> 00:02:47,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

41
00:02:48,679 --> 00:02:49,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, all this is giving the example.

42
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:55,881
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, the situation can be as general as you want, such as, I'm seated.

43
00:02:57,122 --> 00:02:58,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't even have to say it to thinking.

44
00:02:58,822 --> 00:03:00,523
[SPEAKER_00]: You just know already that you're seated.

45
00:03:01,363 --> 00:03:05,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognizing the situation, it's almost like on the level of knowledge.

46
00:03:07,045 --> 00:03:09,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I ask you, what's your body posture now?

47
00:03:10,646 --> 00:03:11,427
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know that.

48
00:03:12,447 --> 00:03:15,308
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't have to get up in order to tell me that you were sitting down.

49
00:03:17,252 --> 00:03:18,873
[SPEAKER_00]: You know you're sitting, so it's already there.

50
00:03:19,213 --> 00:03:20,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to keep it in the mind.

51
00:03:20,875 --> 00:03:29,401
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, over-defining it means making it... approaching it in particular terms, which means you forget about general then.

52
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:35,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Forget about general.

53
00:03:36,866 --> 00:03:42,910
[SPEAKER_02]: But when I think... when I think something about... suppose my situation was with all these people,

54
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:50,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking is particular.

55
00:03:51,203 --> 00:03:52,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it can also be general.

56
00:03:52,745 --> 00:03:53,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends what you think.

57
00:03:53,666 --> 00:03:55,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends how you think.

58
00:03:55,488 --> 00:03:57,890
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, it doesn't mean that particular ceases.

59
00:03:58,511 --> 00:04:03,076
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, particular, in return, makes the general possible.

60
00:04:03,736 --> 00:04:05,178
[SPEAKER_00]: The things that you see particularly,

61
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:09,812
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, oh, it's because of this that my situation has arisen.

62
00:04:10,673 --> 00:04:15,196
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the particular things are defined as such because of your situation.

63
00:04:15,496 --> 00:04:16,476
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like two sticks.

64
00:04:17,037 --> 00:04:22,180
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't conceive them separate from each other.

65
00:04:24,021 --> 00:04:27,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even for an arahant, general and particular, that all remains the same.

66
00:04:27,983 --> 00:04:28,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Really?

67
00:04:28,744 --> 00:04:32,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ignorance in regard to the structure and taking it as mine and everything.

68
00:04:32,986 --> 00:04:33,467
[SPEAKER_00]: That's gone.

69
00:04:35,586 --> 00:04:37,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, of course, yeah, that's how it works.

70
00:04:37,147 --> 00:04:41,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean that you go into general and everything particular disappears.

71
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:42,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

72
00:04:44,852 --> 00:04:50,396
[SPEAKER_02]: General particular is the five aggregates.

73
00:04:50,416 --> 00:04:53,278
[SPEAKER_02]: Putujana holds them, Arhant doesn't.

74
00:05:05,016 --> 00:05:06,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, jhāna, in particular, is the structure.

75
00:05:07,237 --> 00:05:09,178
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same in arahant and in puthujjana.

76
00:05:09,879 --> 00:05:12,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Puthujjana holds it as a result of his ignorance.

77
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:14,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he has greed, aversion, delusion, and everything else.

78
00:05:15,222 --> 00:05:15,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant doesn't.

79
00:05:18,604 --> 00:05:19,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference.

80
00:05:19,524 --> 00:05:26,208
[SPEAKER_00]: But this whole relationship of sitting in the room, while seated, talking, it's the same for arahant or for puthujjana.

81
00:05:26,228 --> 00:05:29,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that puthujjana takes it as mine.

82
00:05:30,467 --> 00:05:36,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, of course, of course.

83
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:44,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's because Bhūtujana takes things in his mind in regard to hunger or thirst, particular or general, he suffers.

84
00:05:46,433 --> 00:05:48,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Ārahant doesn't take his mind, he doesn't suffer.

85
00:05:56,186 --> 00:05:58,507
[SPEAKER_02]: For arahant, there's no self-view.

86
00:05:58,527 --> 00:06:02,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, how could he see anything, something in particular?

87
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:05,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything must become general for arahant.

88
00:06:05,992 --> 00:06:06,332
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

89
00:06:07,333 --> 00:06:08,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, see the point.

90
00:06:08,853 --> 00:06:10,274
[SPEAKER_00]: The fly is going to drink the water.

91
00:06:13,896 --> 00:06:18,419
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only possible for you to attend and go and drink the water because it was available.

92
00:06:19,219 --> 00:06:19,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It was possible.

93
00:06:22,121 --> 00:06:22,821
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that.

94
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:25,082
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the same for an arahant and for a patujana.

95
00:06:25,102 --> 00:06:25,942
[SPEAKER_00]: That makes no difference.

96
00:06:26,622 --> 00:06:28,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That is indifferent to your sense of self.

97
00:06:29,863 --> 00:06:39,625
[SPEAKER_00]: The situation allowed for a drinkable water to be present and then, oh, I'm thirsty and I can drink the water.

98
00:06:39,645 --> 00:06:42,766
[SPEAKER_00]: So, makes no difference whether you take it as mine or not.

99
00:06:43,966 --> 00:06:45,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it didn't come from you.

100
00:06:45,627 --> 00:06:47,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It came from the general situation.

101
00:06:48,412 --> 00:06:51,655
[SPEAKER_02]: Arahant, how does he feel when he sees this?

102
00:06:51,715 --> 00:06:55,539
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's thirsty, he feels significance of thirst.

103
00:06:55,619 --> 00:06:58,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the water will make sense.

104
00:06:58,942 --> 00:07:06,549
[SPEAKER_00]: But he knows that the water and the entire situation originates external to him, nothing to do with him.

105
00:07:08,111 --> 00:07:12,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether he drinks the water or not, or while he drinks the water, he won't think of it.

106
00:07:13,282 --> 00:07:22,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Me and myself, and for me, because he understood the entire situation that already holds this particular drinking water experience in it.

107
00:07:22,848 --> 00:07:24,168
[SPEAKER_00]: He understood that is not self.

108
00:07:26,710 --> 00:07:38,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's very... For me, still, I feel that... Why is it important to think this situation whatever?

109
00:07:38,837 --> 00:07:40,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I...

110
00:07:40,618 --> 00:07:42,179
[SPEAKER_02]: I think whatever it is that

111
00:07:42,955 --> 00:07:58,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Because whatever you can feel, means, like, appearing as a bubble in the mind, whatever, whatever appears, I think if all of them, if I can see clearly, this everything's, without my control appearing everything, isn't, isn't it enough?

112
00:07:59,348 --> 00:08:00,589
[SPEAKER_00]: But are you seeing yourself then?

113
00:08:02,151 --> 00:08:03,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you seeing just what's in front of you?

114
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:11,763
[SPEAKER_02]: These bubbles, if I can see that, cannot support self-view.

115
00:08:12,383 --> 00:08:15,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, you're seeing the bubbles, but what is overseeing you're seeing?

116
00:08:23,090 --> 00:08:24,891
[SPEAKER_02]: It means I'm standing outside.

117
00:08:25,212 --> 00:08:27,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you're assuming yourself outside.

118
00:08:27,373 --> 00:08:27,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

119
00:08:28,674 --> 00:08:31,916
[SPEAKER_00]: But by assuming these things in front of you, you put yourself out.

120
00:08:33,317 --> 00:08:35,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you don't really include your sense of self there.

121
00:08:37,172 --> 00:08:38,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is seeing, there are bubbles.

122
00:08:39,434 --> 00:08:40,535
[SPEAKER_00]: There are bubbles being seen.

123
00:08:41,055 --> 00:08:44,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're seeing everything, but you're not seeing the nature of seeing.

124
00:08:48,401 --> 00:08:54,446
[SPEAKER_02]: If I could think that, only I can think as myself, as self-view, this is myself.

125
00:08:54,546 --> 00:08:59,870
[SPEAKER_02]: If I can think that these bubbles, only I can think, regard as mine.

126
00:09:00,726 --> 00:09:04,827
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, my, my, my, I am, it's included in these bubbles.

127
00:09:04,967 --> 00:09:09,948
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, only because that only could cause... No, not quite, no, no.

128
00:09:10,408 --> 00:09:20,310
[SPEAKER_00]: No, see, because when you say, the fact that bubbles already arise for you, no matter how you think them afterwards, the sense of self was already there.

129
00:09:21,730 --> 00:09:25,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just managing the situation as opposed to uprooting it.

130
00:09:26,554 --> 00:09:28,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but self... you actually...

131
00:09:28,254 --> 00:09:31,735
[SPEAKER_02]: I never see any other... really appearing as a bubble.

132
00:09:31,775 --> 00:09:34,296
[SPEAKER_02]: These are actually calling as mine, not I am.

133
00:09:34,436 --> 00:09:35,176
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you will not.

134
00:09:35,556 --> 00:09:36,296
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't see that.

135
00:09:36,316 --> 00:09:45,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it's... if the sense of self and sense of mind was to appear right in front of you so clearly, you wouldn't need a Buddha to become an Arahant.

136
00:09:46,658 --> 00:09:49,559
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, what else I'm calling myself?

137
00:09:50,819 --> 00:09:52,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that depends, really.

138
00:09:52,159 --> 00:09:54,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You can call any of the aggregates myself.

139
00:09:56,535 --> 00:10:01,659
[SPEAKER_00]: And the thing is, you expect to find a particular thing that you called myself.

140
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:06,062
[SPEAKER_00]: But as I said, my sense of self is never particular, it's always general.

141
00:10:06,763 --> 00:10:10,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by refusing to look at the general level, you'll never see the sense of self.

142
00:10:12,147 --> 00:10:14,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you expect to find it on the particular level.

143
00:10:15,229 --> 00:10:16,210
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't appear there.

144
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:18,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no wonder you can't find your sense of self anywhere.

145
00:10:20,112 --> 00:10:24,796
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that we can talk about your sense of self, means there is something being designated as such.

146
00:10:26,035 --> 00:10:31,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Something very ambiguous, unclear, general, non-particular.

147
00:10:32,096 --> 00:10:35,877
[SPEAKER_00]: And as such, it is real, it is present as a phenomenon.

148
00:10:37,717 --> 00:10:41,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to start seeing it like that, in order to be able to understand it like that.

149
00:10:42,138 --> 00:10:51,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just used to seeing things in front of you, bubbles, particular, sense of self never appears like that, which is why it's sense of self.

150
00:10:52,239 --> 00:10:53,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you can never see it.

151
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:55,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see,

152
00:10:56,357 --> 00:11:04,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Your general situation, which is where sense of self is, you see that as fully determined by these particular random bubbles.

153
00:11:04,959 --> 00:11:14,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Indirectly, then you're already, like, uprooting your sense of self without directly looking at it, because you can't.

154
00:11:14,781 --> 00:11:18,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you look directly, your sense of self is behind your look.

155
00:11:19,342 --> 00:11:20,062
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it works.

156
00:11:20,629 --> 00:11:24,110
[SPEAKER_02]: It means you need to see the relation, how these bubbles appeared.

157
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:25,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, that's not the relation.

158
00:11:25,890 --> 00:11:27,011
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that's the explanation.

159
00:11:27,811 --> 00:11:31,111
[SPEAKER_00]: How the bubbles appeared would be an explanation.

160
00:11:31,171 --> 00:11:33,992
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that bubbles are there, means you're in the situation.

161
00:11:36,493 --> 00:11:39,333
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you're in the situation, means bubble can appear as bubble.

162
00:11:43,214 --> 00:11:45,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, sense yourself lingering somewhere in between.

163
00:11:47,127 --> 00:11:50,450
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can never look at it directly, because it's always behind your look.

164
00:11:51,571 --> 00:11:55,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you learn to know where it is, without needing to look.

165
00:11:58,356 --> 00:11:59,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Because looking will hide it.

166
00:12:01,299 --> 00:12:06,223
[SPEAKER_02]: It is for me not able to see what I'm calling myself.

167
00:12:06,363 --> 00:12:08,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Not directly, not as a particular thing.

168
00:12:08,345 --> 00:12:08,525
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

169
00:12:09,986 --> 00:12:10,747
[SPEAKER_00]: You can know it.

170
00:12:11,598 --> 00:12:13,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Indirectly, where your sense of self is.

171
00:12:14,441 --> 00:12:17,404
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't look at it directly, because the look comes from the sense of self.

172
00:12:22,830 --> 00:12:28,176
[SPEAKER_02]: I was feeling that whatever I feel inside the body or in the mind,

173
00:12:28,967 --> 00:12:34,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Those things, actually, I was thinking as mine, myself, my feelings, my thought, everything.

174
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:38,152
[SPEAKER_02]: These, these all, like, all combined together.

175
00:12:38,212 --> 00:12:40,233
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking, this here I am.

176
00:12:40,313 --> 00:12:48,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, like, even those things, like, your... the sense of mind is in regard to the things.

177
00:12:48,778 --> 00:12:50,359
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is not in the things.

178
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:57,523
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it's your attitude towards certain things that is the attitude of mind.

179
00:12:59,510 --> 00:13:01,030
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that doesn't mean that everything is yours.

180
00:13:04,071 --> 00:13:07,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Just means that some things... it's enough for some things to be regarded as mine.

181
00:13:07,592 --> 00:13:08,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of self is there.

182
00:13:11,253 --> 00:13:14,474
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense of self is there.

183
00:13:14,714 --> 00:13:15,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you...

184
00:13:15,074 --> 00:13:19,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the sense of mind doesn't appear clearly right in front of you.

185
00:13:19,216 --> 00:13:22,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also general in regard to a particular thing that you took as mine.

186
00:13:23,377 --> 00:13:23,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitude.

187
00:13:25,194 --> 00:13:25,975
[SPEAKER_00]: What is attitude?

188
00:13:26,835 --> 00:13:28,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you see your attitude particularly?

189
00:13:29,097 --> 00:13:29,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, here it is.

190
00:13:30,077 --> 00:13:30,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

191
00:13:30,658 --> 00:13:32,619
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an attitude in regard to things.

192
00:13:39,463 --> 00:13:40,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a mental phenomenon.

193
00:13:42,585 --> 00:13:43,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Or just a phenomenon.

194
00:13:44,546 --> 00:13:44,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma.

195
00:13:49,950 --> 00:13:51,351
[SPEAKER_02]: But it is always there.

196
00:13:51,631 --> 00:13:52,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything else.

197
00:13:52,612 --> 00:13:53,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

198
00:13:54,469 --> 00:13:57,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean that it's permanent, but means it's in regard to everything.

199
00:13:57,091 --> 00:14:02,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're looking at things in front of you, you're not seeing your attitude in regard to those things.

200
00:14:02,955 --> 00:14:04,816
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just seeing what's in front of you.

201
00:14:05,897 --> 00:14:06,778
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's wrong.

202
00:14:07,278 --> 00:14:08,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You leave your attitude out.

203
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:12,602
[SPEAKER_00]: But your attitude is that which needs to be changed, not the things.

204
00:14:13,002 --> 00:14:14,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were not a problem in the first place.

205
00:14:16,184 --> 00:14:17,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the bubbles, they're all bubbles, whatever.

206
00:14:18,526 --> 00:14:21,848
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's your attitude in regard to them that you need to change, not them.

207
00:14:22,633 --> 00:14:30,056
[SPEAKER_02]: Puputujana, always looking at everything, like a... based on self-view, everything... No, no, no.

208
00:14:30,897 --> 00:14:31,837
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not everything.

209
00:14:31,857 --> 00:14:41,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It's me, me, I, and... Putujana takes his... takes not-self with self, or takes self with not-self, or takes self with self.

210
00:14:42,622 --> 00:14:46,344
[SPEAKER_00]: But he cannot do... what he cannot do is take not-self with not-self.

211
00:14:51,611 --> 00:14:53,312
[SPEAKER_02]: This is something like...

212
00:14:53,452 --> 00:14:55,633
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm supposed to be looking at something.

213
00:14:56,273 --> 00:14:58,674
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything I'm thinking, I am looking at it.

214
00:14:58,915 --> 00:15:01,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Arahant, but maybe he's not thinking, he is looking at it.

215
00:15:02,216 --> 00:15:11,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, in a way you can say that, but he can think I am looking at this, but the thought of I am looking at this is not his.

216
00:15:12,041 --> 00:15:12,741
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not I.

217
00:15:13,001 --> 00:15:13,602
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not me.

218
00:15:13,642 --> 00:15:14,542
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no conceit.

219
00:15:15,723 --> 00:15:16,323
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all gone.

220
00:15:16,903 --> 00:15:18,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So then it's just the designation I am.

221
00:15:25,151 --> 00:15:26,472
[SPEAKER_02]: What if I do?

222
00:15:26,512 --> 00:15:42,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I was thinking, I am doing everything, looking at... No, that's like when you're trying to, like, force, force the view over it, I am this, I am this, but no, you have to find yourself within that situation, and that's how you abandon it.

223
00:15:44,227 --> 00:15:52,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you're producing things in front of you constantly, you are doing that, which means that you, which is doing it, is out, it's not included there.

224
00:15:54,282 --> 00:15:55,342
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it can never work.

225
00:16:01,465 --> 00:16:07,487
[SPEAKER_02]: But for Patujana, it's not a temporal phenomenon, like a self-view is always there.

226
00:16:07,848 --> 00:16:13,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it should, in regard to the most fundamental aspects of the Agra, it's, yeah, it's always the same, which applies to Patujana.

227
00:16:15,092 --> 00:16:18,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say the most fundamental, I mean the most general.

228
00:16:19,275 --> 00:16:29,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he might not be taking this particular flask, carpet, cushion as mine, but he's taking the whole general situation, his body at least, as mine.

229
00:16:29,883 --> 00:16:36,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, whatever situation his body ends up being in, he'll imply his sense of self in it.

230
00:16:38,008 --> 00:16:42,972
[SPEAKER_00]: And whether through for me or not for me, or me, or both, or neither.

231
00:16:44,117 --> 00:16:52,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it needs to be undone in that indirect manner, through undoing the situation.

232
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:56,601
[SPEAKER_02]: To see things, actually, generally, is like something...

233
00:16:56,701 --> 00:17:02,463
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to put something outside, you know, to see something, things, like... No, it's not the answer.

234
00:17:02,483 --> 00:17:08,505
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to discern it, where it is, how it is, without having to move it about in order to understand it.

235
00:17:11,145 --> 00:17:16,086
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent the thing has arisen, that's how and that's where you have to understand it.

236
00:17:19,527 --> 00:17:20,408
[SPEAKER_00]: That's discernment.

237
00:17:20,588 --> 00:17:23,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning what's given.

238
00:17:23,328 --> 00:17:29,550
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing's given, but you have to discern within that what's given, how, like, what's important, what's impermanent, and so on.

239
00:17:37,722 --> 00:17:42,863
[SPEAKER_02]: It's very new, actually.

240
00:17:44,623 --> 00:17:47,684
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't really know how much of this is now.

241
00:17:48,444 --> 00:17:51,025
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, yeah, it's within the same ideas.

242
00:17:52,385 --> 00:17:52,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

243
00:17:53,225 --> 00:17:54,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's from the suttas.

244
00:17:54,685 --> 00:17:55,886
[SPEAKER_00]: And he didn't invent anything.

245
00:17:56,166 --> 00:17:57,146
[SPEAKER_02]: From the sutta?

246
00:17:57,166 --> 00:17:57,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

247
00:17:59,266 --> 00:18:00,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Really?

248
00:18:00,367 --> 00:18:02,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you read, you'll see all of this.

249
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:05,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Explanations you can find in the suttas.

250
00:18:05,801 --> 00:18:18,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, the suttas being so general, like, you know, Bhikkhu Bodhi calls the suttas to support his three-life interpretation, because he finds parts that have nothing to do with bodhichitta and pada, but he would call them for bodhichitta and pada.

251
00:18:19,929 --> 00:18:25,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when he needs to, he would ignore the abstract principle just to support the view.

252
00:18:26,894 --> 00:18:32,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And the suttas allow that because they're so profound and so general in their description that, actually,

253
00:18:33,261 --> 00:18:41,324
[SPEAKER_00]: People can use them and misuse them.

254
00:18:41,344 --> 00:18:45,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, like, if you start thinking like this, then you can start reading the suttas in this way.

255
00:18:45,605 --> 00:18:47,086
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they will read very differently.

256
00:18:47,526 --> 00:18:52,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it will become apparent why, like, one sutta is enough for arahantry, if you fully understand it.

257
00:18:52,107 --> 00:18:54,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Before you were reading sutta in different way.

258
00:18:55,269 --> 00:18:57,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, before I was reading suttas like everybody else does.

259
00:18:58,410 --> 00:18:59,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, kind of...

260
00:19:00,790 --> 00:19:08,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Wise inspiration, but kind of product of folklore India, long time ago, ancient text type.

261
00:19:08,453 --> 00:19:09,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But then I started reading in the Veera.

262
00:19:10,013 --> 00:19:19,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then I started reading existential philosophy that was making lots of sense, in terms of intention, action, responsibility, feelings, anxiety, and so on.

263
00:19:19,996 --> 00:19:21,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I started reading in the Veera.

264
00:19:22,505 --> 00:19:41,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Talking about the suttas in those terms, and I realized, wow, you can actually... yes, you can read it as an ancient sacred text that just produced poetic inspiration, but you can also read it as an existential phenomenological description of experience right here, right now.

265
00:19:42,379 --> 00:19:46,181
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stop and think which of those two ways, either romantic inspiration,

266
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:57,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a phenomenological, accurate, actual description of your experience, which one of those two is most likely to make you an arahant, you realize it's the latter one.

267
00:19:58,303 --> 00:20:09,206
[SPEAKER_00]: If you read as a description of the universal aspects of the experience, whether two and a half thousand years ago, whether now, whether two and a half thousand years later, still the same universal experience, you understand it like that.

268
00:20:09,586 --> 00:20:10,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, of course.

269
00:20:10,866 --> 00:20:14,107
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need is one sutta to become an arahant, if you fully understand it.

270
00:20:15,138 --> 00:20:21,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's been spoken two millennia ago, or now, or ten millennia late, just makes no difference.

271
00:20:21,422 --> 00:20:23,103
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing spoken.

272
00:20:23,123 --> 00:20:29,988
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said, when he discovered the dhamma, he realized, oh, it's like finding an ancient ancient path that was already there.

273
00:20:30,849 --> 00:20:33,451
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't invent himself nothing.

274
00:20:36,192 --> 00:20:36,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's...

275
00:20:37,602 --> 00:20:51,720
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I say to people, like, you know, you might... you don't want to get... you don't need to get into all the philosophy and everything everybody talks about, but if you... at least if you get the attitude towards the suttas from him, you can't ask for more.

276
00:20:51,740 --> 00:20:53,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how to read them, how to approach them.

277
00:20:54,609 --> 00:20:55,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's very interesting.

278
00:20:55,870 --> 00:20:59,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because then you read the suttas and you stop and think about every single sentence.

279
00:20:59,994 --> 00:21:08,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, you read, oh, consciousness and da-da-da, and then, well, do I even know, putting the sutta aside, in my own terms, what consciousness is?

280
00:21:08,543 --> 00:21:08,923
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't.

281
00:21:09,204 --> 00:21:16,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can I expect that that which I think consciousness is, is that which the Buddha talks about when he says consciousness?

282
00:21:17,087 --> 00:21:26,394
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you stop and think further, and you realize, well, if my thought of consciousness would correspond to Buddha's thought of consciousness, I would be an arahant, or at least a sotāpanna.

283
00:21:27,035 --> 00:21:31,678
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm neither of those, therefore, when I think consciousness, that's not the consciousness that Buddha talks about.

284
00:21:32,199 --> 00:21:34,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So you keep drilling, and drilling, and drilling.

285
00:21:35,902 --> 00:21:37,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even if you did it, you'll come back to it.

286
00:21:38,383 --> 00:21:39,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And you read it again.

287
00:21:42,226 --> 00:21:42,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Many times.

288
00:21:44,886 --> 00:21:45,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Very interesting.

289
00:21:46,106 --> 00:21:49,187
[SPEAKER_02]: At the moment, they can't explain in such a way.

290
00:21:49,207 --> 00:21:50,368
[SPEAKER_02]: It's very interesting.

291
00:21:50,388 --> 00:21:50,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Wow.

292
00:21:52,048 --> 00:21:52,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't know.

293
00:21:52,908 --> 00:22:00,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I haven't heard anybody speak like this before, like, in these ten years, so... You had no teacher, no, you said?

294
00:22:00,851 --> 00:22:01,671
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no.

295
00:22:01,831 --> 00:22:02,251
[SPEAKER_00]: No teacher.

296
00:22:02,271 --> 00:22:07,493
[SPEAKER_00]: But I haven't even heard anybody resembling, like, this type of approach when they speak.

297
00:22:08,713 --> 00:22:09,954
[SPEAKER_00]: This way of thinking, so...

298
00:22:12,914 --> 00:22:14,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not surprising that you haven't...

299
00:22:17,705 --> 00:22:20,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Only through, through your experience and through your meditation.

300
00:22:20,666 --> 00:22:29,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, through experience, through meditation, through the suttas, through, through, through pondering and understanding, you know, you, you, you, you, you get to see where people come from when they talk about it.

301
00:22:31,310 --> 00:22:44,656
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh, I haven't, yeah, I haven't really heard anybody even coming remotely from this direction, the direction that Navira was talking about, the direction that's implied in the suttas.

302
00:22:45,559 --> 00:22:48,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that there aren't any, but just I haven't heard in the West where I live.

303
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,422
[SPEAKER_02]: There are some monks, they don't agree with the sutra.

304
00:22:52,462 --> 00:22:55,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Some of them, they say that some of them are wrong.

305
00:22:55,843 --> 00:22:57,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, exactly.

306
00:22:57,083 --> 00:22:58,484
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, well, that's the thing.

307
00:22:58,764 --> 00:23:02,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It was so... such a long time ago when they were spoken.

308
00:23:02,125 --> 00:23:05,406
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can't prove, you can't disapprove either.

309
00:23:06,867 --> 00:23:10,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people come across some parts, they can't come to terms with it.

310
00:23:10,768 --> 00:23:12,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, they must have been introduced later.

311
00:23:13,605 --> 00:23:15,565
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, yeah... How do you feel?

312
00:23:15,665 --> 00:23:16,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you agree with it?

313
00:23:16,386 --> 00:23:17,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't care.

314
00:23:17,226 --> 00:23:18,426
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's it's kind of irrelevant.

315
00:23:18,766 --> 00:23:28,588
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's it's a silly thing to do, because unless you're a sotāpanna, you have no criteria of what's right, what's wrong, which is why you're not a sotāpanna.

316
00:23:29,428 --> 00:23:37,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, when you choose to ignore certain parts because you believe that they were introduced or whatever else, well, you're following your patujana's criteria.

317
00:23:39,010 --> 00:23:43,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's, again, you don't know for sure, but to be on the safe side,

318
00:23:44,661 --> 00:23:48,923
[SPEAKER_00]: You take it all, you accept it all, just so that you can practice and hopefully get the right view.

319
00:23:48,983 --> 00:23:51,845
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get the right view, then certain things might become more apparent.

320
00:23:52,545 --> 00:23:55,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't really know whether this came later or not.

321
00:23:55,747 --> 00:23:59,028
[SPEAKER_00]: But if there are such parts, they won't pertain to the dhamma.

322
00:24:00,869 --> 00:24:04,991
[SPEAKER_00]: It might pertain to some story or some social relationship or something.

323
00:24:05,292 --> 00:24:10,494
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not pertain to the four noble truths and the concept, the main concept.

324
00:24:10,514 --> 00:24:11,195
[SPEAKER_00]: It just won't.

325
00:24:11,715 --> 00:24:13,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it does, you will know this is wrong.

326
00:24:13,236 --> 00:24:14,016
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not the Buddha.

327
00:24:16,661 --> 00:24:20,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, when you read Mahāyāna Sūtras, there's not a single Buddha word there.

328
00:24:20,942 --> 00:24:21,302
[SPEAKER_02]: Really?

329
00:24:21,742 --> 00:24:29,024
[SPEAKER_00]: It was stuff I read, at least, the Huineng and the Six Pārtya, Six Pārtya Huineng and Heart Sutra and stuff.

330
00:24:29,204 --> 00:24:31,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just very strange.

331
00:24:33,725 --> 00:24:34,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It was...

332
00:24:34,505 --> 00:24:40,446
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's known, it's not debatable that it's introduced later by their own teachers and preachers.

333
00:24:40,986 --> 00:24:43,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, you know, there can be things introduced later

334
00:24:44,962 --> 00:24:47,223
[SPEAKER_00]: New arahants coming and teaching in their own terms.

335
00:24:47,863 --> 00:24:50,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It will still be the same dhamma.

336
00:24:51,525 --> 00:24:53,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it isn't, it means it's not.

337
00:24:53,566 --> 00:24:54,286
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not arahants.

338
00:24:55,747 --> 00:24:57,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and, and, you know, you can see that.

339
00:24:57,588 --> 00:24:58,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see...

340
00:24:58,788 --> 00:24:59,769
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a problem.

341
00:24:59,909 --> 00:25:08,533
[SPEAKER_02]: One day, the Venerable Bhārata, he told me that if someone, any monk, disagrees in the sutra, then that's, that's, you must avoid such... Well, no, it depends.

342
00:25:08,873 --> 00:25:12,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if they're disagreeing, if they say, if they're disagreeing with the key concepts,

343
00:25:13,809 --> 00:25:18,651
[SPEAKER_00]: If they're dismissing Pratītyasena Upādāna, like Bhikkhu Bodhi, yeah, you should dismiss him then.

344
00:25:19,972 --> 00:25:25,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't have to dismiss his scholarship, but his views, you should really consider them.

345
00:25:25,374 --> 00:25:37,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, as I said, even without, you know, needing to be a sotāpanna or something, if you stop and think about it, well, even as an abstract principle of Pratītyasena Upādāna, it's still a principle within which the whole...

346
00:25:38,019 --> 00:25:42,481
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason it's abstract, it's because a person does not see directly.

347
00:25:44,218 --> 00:25:50,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if anything, if you call something abstract principle, means that's something you didn't understand, which is why it is abstract.

348
00:25:50,822 --> 00:25:54,885
[SPEAKER_00]: It's abstract only in regard to its content, not in its nature.

349
00:25:55,305 --> 00:25:56,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, principle is principle.

350
00:25:57,987 --> 00:26:01,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, oh, that principle is abstract for me, because I don't understand it.

351
00:26:03,331 --> 00:26:12,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if everybody starts with this principle, and then you have somebody comes and dismisses the principle and gives you a different explanation, you should be doubtful of that.

352
00:26:13,620 --> 00:26:18,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if somebody goes even further and blatantly says, no, I disagree with that sutta.

353
00:26:19,225 --> 00:26:21,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, samādhiti sutta, I think that's all wrong.

354
00:26:22,668 --> 00:26:35,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you should, like, you shouldn't waste your time speaking to them then, if they say such thing, because samādhiti sutta does not pertain to any traditional, any cultural, any product of the, you know, ancient India.

355
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:38,063
[SPEAKER_00]: It pertains to the Right View and the Noble Eightfold Path.

356
00:26:39,531 --> 00:26:44,113
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody dismisses that, well, they're not really following the Buddha's teaching, even if they're monks.

357
00:26:44,493 --> 00:26:48,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but there's no Buddha's teaching if someone doesn't agree with it.

358
00:26:48,575 --> 00:26:49,375
[SPEAKER_02]: It's okay.

359
00:26:49,415 --> 00:26:50,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's different.

360
00:26:50,476 --> 00:26:56,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what they're not agreeing, they're not agreeing with an interpretation of the Buddha's teaching.

361
00:26:57,118 --> 00:27:00,080
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't agree with the view of the Buddha's teaching.

362
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:02,941
[SPEAKER_02]: You say some are here, they don't agree.

363
00:27:02,961 --> 00:27:03,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

364
00:27:03,861 --> 00:27:04,681
[SPEAKER_00]: No, see, that's the thing.

365
00:27:04,722 --> 00:27:07,823
[SPEAKER_00]: They might disagree or they might not be able to comprehend.

366
00:27:08,683 --> 00:27:22,771
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the things Nyanavira was saying, but I think with little effort, they would understand the basic ideas, and they would know whether Nyanavira was or wasn't a Sotāpanna, if they were truly artists.

367
00:27:23,872 --> 00:27:36,899
[SPEAKER_00]: But people often dismiss it entirely, while they're still... they know they're Putuginas, but they don't know that as Putuginas, they cannot know what's right, what's wrong.

368
00:27:37,992 --> 00:27:50,134
[SPEAKER_00]: I met quite a lot of monks like that, senior monks like that, who would know, who would openly say, oh, I haven't attained anything, but that wouldn't stop them from saying what you should do in terms of practice, which is a contradiction in terms.

369
00:27:50,875 --> 00:27:59,916
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know yourself what you need to do in terms of to get the right view, how can you even conceive teaching somebody else to get the right view?

370
00:28:01,257 --> 00:28:04,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just mind-boggling contradiction that doesn't seem to stop them.

371
00:28:05,618 --> 00:28:07,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, coming from the same place,

372
00:28:08,539 --> 00:28:20,826
[SPEAKER_00]: If they come across something they don't like, they don't like the way it was put out, they don't like the way it was taught, displeases them or whatever, such as in Navidā's books and his views, they'll dismiss it.

373
00:28:21,567 --> 00:28:26,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing themselves they're putujana and they have no criteria for the true dismissal, they'll dismiss it.

374
00:28:26,810 --> 00:28:28,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why they stay putujanas.

375
00:28:29,752 --> 00:28:29,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

376
00:28:30,172 --> 00:28:30,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

377
00:28:30,872 --> 00:28:31,733
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just how it works.

378
00:28:32,673 --> 00:28:32,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

379
00:28:34,652 --> 00:28:37,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but you don't know what's right, you don't know what's wrong.

380
00:28:37,613 --> 00:28:39,633
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the element of faith is necessary.

381
00:28:41,014 --> 00:28:47,015
[SPEAKER_00]: But that faith should be, like, governed or guided or directed by intelligence.

382
00:28:47,115 --> 00:28:56,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just blind faith, where you accept everything called Buddhism, but you discern and you think what is least likely to be wrong.

383
00:28:56,898 --> 00:28:57,698
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what's right.

384
00:28:58,399 --> 00:28:59,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything might be wrong.

385
00:29:00,193 --> 00:29:05,196
[SPEAKER_00]: What is least likely to be wrong, and then I'll start putting my effort in that.

386
00:29:06,336 --> 00:29:18,082
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if it's... if I was a little bit fortunate, if I was intelligent enough, I might have chosen the right thing, and with a sufficient amount of effort I put, I'll get a confirmation that it's the right thing.

387
00:29:19,082 --> 00:29:21,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why in the suttas the Buddha would refer to

388
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:26,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Satipaṭṭhāna is somebody who has a confirmed confidence in dhamma.

389
00:29:27,424 --> 00:29:31,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they were confident, oh, this is least, this is most likely the correct thing.

390
00:29:32,049 --> 00:29:35,493
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, they had that thought confirmed when they became satipaṭṭhāna.

391
00:29:35,513 --> 00:29:36,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they know that they know.

392
00:29:45,521 --> 00:29:54,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he... the faith gets replaced by confidence.

393
00:29:54,744 --> 00:29:57,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It all becomes unshakable faith, which is not really faith anymore.

394
00:29:57,645 --> 00:29:58,366
[SPEAKER_00]: It's confidence.

395
00:29:58,426 --> 00:29:59,426
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows that he knows.

396
00:30:00,366 --> 00:30:04,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And he cannot even conceive, because the Buddha is the dhamma.

397
00:30:04,728 --> 00:30:05,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma is the Buddha.

398
00:30:05,488 --> 00:30:11,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not... it's not that the Buddha is the person or something who happened to say something right.

399
00:30:11,310 --> 00:30:11,510
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

400
00:30:11,530 --> 00:30:12,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha is that teaching.

401
00:30:13,560 --> 00:30:15,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they understood the teaching, they understood the Buddha.

402
00:30:16,982 --> 00:30:20,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's inconceivable for them to now follow a different teacher.

403
00:30:21,124 --> 00:30:28,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say different teacher, I don't mean another monk who might be teaching the same, and then they have, OK, I'll listen to you a bit more, because you seem like they did follow Sariputta.

404
00:30:29,829 --> 00:30:41,895
[SPEAKER_00]: No, when I say different teacher, I mean Christian, teaching Christian philosophy, Christian views, or Western science, for that matter, because that's almost like a religion today, or psychology.

405
00:30:41,935 --> 00:30:43,196
[SPEAKER_00]: They wouldn't be able to adopt that.

406
00:30:44,286 --> 00:30:45,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a wrong view.

407
00:30:47,588 --> 00:30:53,050
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way they could adopt any other teaching, if it's a teaching that's the same as the dharma, which would be the dharma.

408
00:30:53,750 --> 00:30:54,731
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually interesting.

409
00:30:54,751 --> 00:30:58,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Some Christians, they can become a real... Not through Christianity.

410
00:30:58,613 --> 00:30:58,673
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

411
00:30:58,753 --> 00:30:59,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Not through Christianity.

412
00:30:59,753 --> 00:30:59,953
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

413
00:31:00,273 --> 00:31:11,979
[SPEAKER_02]: I heard Venerable Pāṇḍa told me that when Buddha was alive, at that time, they were Sotāpanna or something, and in this life, they were born as Christian or something, at least in Christian family.

414
00:31:12,673 --> 00:31:16,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, eh... something, anyway... No, that makes no difference.

415
00:31:16,895 --> 00:31:21,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what view your family holds, and what heritage you're born in, that's irrelevant.

416
00:31:22,016 --> 00:31:22,176
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

417
00:31:22,396 --> 00:31:28,919
[SPEAKER_00]: What view you hold, and what teaching you follow, personally, to try to understand, that's what matters.

418
00:31:29,519 --> 00:31:31,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody can might well be a Christian priest.

419
00:31:32,140 --> 00:31:37,802
[SPEAKER_00]: If he ponders on impermanence, and suffering, and nature of not-self, he ponders on dhamma.

420
00:31:38,798 --> 00:31:44,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he becomes an ārya, he becomes because of the dhamma, not because of his believing imaginary friend.

421
00:31:45,322 --> 00:31:53,046
[SPEAKER_02]: After... if they become ārya, with being the Christian, all of those beliefs that the God... Well, they would see them as wrong.

422
00:31:54,067 --> 00:31:54,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Wrong?

423
00:31:54,447 --> 00:31:54,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

424
00:31:56,128 --> 00:32:00,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not even... like, they would see them as irrelevant.

425
00:32:01,091 --> 00:32:01,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

426
00:32:02,271 --> 00:32:07,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the belief in God and everything, it's... not as much as it's wrong, but it's just irrelevant.

427
00:32:07,214 --> 00:32:08,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes no difference.

428
00:32:09,908 --> 00:32:23,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it makes difference for people who don't know and put their whole life in those beliefs, but if somebody understood the nature of suffering and freedom from it, belief... it just doesn't even apply, belief, in God anymore.

429
00:32:23,838 --> 00:32:29,482
[SPEAKER_02]: In Seattle, a Buddhist country with many people, they believe in astrology and the horoscope and everything.

430
00:32:29,843 --> 00:32:30,443
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the problem?

431
00:32:30,463 --> 00:32:30,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

432
00:32:30,663 --> 00:32:31,224
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the problem?

433
00:32:31,244 --> 00:32:32,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's a superstition.

434
00:32:32,325 --> 00:32:37,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the most kind of primal attitude towards, you know, the unknown.

435
00:32:38,029 --> 00:32:39,130
[SPEAKER_00]: People are superstitious.

436
00:32:40,051 --> 00:32:43,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Magic and like, oh, omens and good signs and bad signs.

437
00:32:43,473 --> 00:32:43,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

438
00:32:44,234 --> 00:32:46,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically like a

439
00:32:48,853 --> 00:32:54,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Something people need to do in order to to quickly escape anxiety.

440
00:32:54,875 --> 00:32:57,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety of an existing individual.

441
00:32:58,637 --> 00:33:07,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, just being alive already means you're in a perilous situation, because you can die at any time and you have absolutely no say in it.

442
00:33:08,466 --> 00:33:17,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And just that single thought can freak people up, out, can make them mad, if they really... Like you... you just... you couldn't stop it.

443
00:33:17,350 --> 00:33:21,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole world can end in a second, and you can't do anything about it.

444
00:33:21,853 --> 00:33:27,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the immediate response to that is, no, but it will all be good.

445
00:33:27,916 --> 00:33:28,436
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll believe.

446
00:33:29,957 --> 00:33:35,860
[SPEAKER_00]: God will protect me, or universal energy will protect me, or my horoscope tell me I'll be fortunate.

447
00:33:36,618 --> 00:33:43,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything like that, just to... just give it a little grain of hope to overcompensate and allows you enough to not look at the anxiety.

448
00:33:44,222 --> 00:33:46,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you keep looking, you'll go mad.

449
00:33:46,724 --> 00:33:48,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha did say that.

450
00:33:48,385 --> 00:34:01,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he said that if somebody experienced suffering rightly, correctly, that anxiety, open, clear, without any cover of the views and superstition and all sorts of other religions, there are only two outcomes.

451
00:34:01,933 --> 00:34:05,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Mental derangement or search.

452
00:34:06,492 --> 00:34:17,416
[SPEAKER_00]: A noble search, they will bring that pain, and it would... like, if somebody is strong enough to not go mad, it would result in them looking for a medicine that can remove this pain.

453
00:34:19,196 --> 00:34:21,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the state the person needs to be in.

454
00:34:22,337 --> 00:34:32,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, he needs to be open to the existential anxiety to such an extent that, yeah, there is a risk of madness, but that means there is also a possibility of the right search.

455
00:34:33,968 --> 00:34:44,913
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean the person actually has to go mad, but means the person has to stop covering it up through superstition, through horoscope, through empty beliefs, through magic, and so on.

456
00:34:45,233 --> 00:34:46,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Mental weakness.

457
00:34:46,714 --> 00:34:48,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is conflicting.

458
00:34:48,635 --> 00:34:49,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one?

459
00:34:49,776 --> 00:34:53,017
[SPEAKER_01]: The astrology of things.

460
00:34:53,477 --> 00:34:58,700
[SPEAKER_01]: When he... when he got... Oh, yeah, the auspicious signs.

461
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:02,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he didn't refuse it completely in terms of...

462
00:35:03,958 --> 00:35:04,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, that's it.

463
00:35:04,579 --> 00:35:06,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's always 50-50, isn't it?

464
00:35:06,161 --> 00:35:08,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it means 5 out of 10 can always get it right.

465
00:35:19,163 --> 00:35:23,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I say this will happen or that will happen, well, one of the two outcomes will happen.

466
00:35:23,984 --> 00:35:26,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I have 50% of chance of being right.

467
00:35:26,485 --> 00:35:36,067
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha never dismissed that, but he never ever used it as a... like, in reference to... pertain to the core of the teaching and feelings of saṃsāra or whatever else.

468
00:35:37,127 --> 00:35:41,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that also, like, begs the question, well, how did he use it then?

469
00:35:41,509 --> 00:35:42,109
[SPEAKER_00]: In what sense?

470
00:35:43,169 --> 00:35:46,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it was like a real divine... Like, there is... there are people who could

471
00:35:47,758 --> 00:35:55,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Even back in the day, who could develop, like, supernatural abilities, and they could see a future destination based on the current actions you're doing.

472
00:35:57,547 --> 00:36:04,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there are people who can see your two or three next destinations based on the current actions you're doing.

473
00:36:04,152 --> 00:36:05,193
[SPEAKER_00]: There are people who can see ten.

474
00:36:06,254 --> 00:36:11,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, and then if they say, well, this is your, like,

475
00:36:12,318 --> 00:36:14,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Future projection, then that's true.

476
00:36:15,039 --> 00:36:16,760
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's not set in stone.

477
00:36:17,500 --> 00:36:20,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you change your behavior, you can change your future outcome.

478
00:36:21,722 --> 00:36:29,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Like with the current set of mental determinations and things you do and whether actions are wholesome and unwholesome, you are heading to heaven.

479
00:36:30,585 --> 00:36:35,427
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that changes, if you start misbehaving and doing some unwholesome stuff, you won't be going to heaven.

480
00:36:36,068 --> 00:36:37,128
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha said.

481
00:36:37,148 --> 00:36:39,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes people see only one life ahead.

482
00:36:40,517 --> 00:36:47,104
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they say... and then they see somebody here misbehaving, but they see his next life is heaven.

483
00:36:48,165 --> 00:36:52,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they see somebody behaving, but they see his next life is also heaven.

484
00:36:53,031 --> 00:36:57,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they say, oh, makes no difference whether you behave or you misbehave, you're reborn in heaven.

485
00:36:58,737 --> 00:37:01,780
[SPEAKER_00]: But then another person comes and sees five lives ahead.

486
00:37:02,627 --> 00:37:09,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he says, well, yeah, he might be in the heaven next life, because actually in the previous life he did some good stuff, but next four lives are misery.

487
00:37:10,589 --> 00:37:13,050
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who behaves, all five will be heaven.

488
00:37:13,670 --> 00:37:17,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he says, no, it matters how you behave, whether good or bad.

489
00:37:18,311 --> 00:37:26,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even those views are limited, but they all pertain to the external, like, nature of saṃsāra, what might be ahead of you, and so on.

490
00:37:27,071 --> 00:37:30,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to the way to escape it, which is what the Buddha was concerned about.

491
00:37:30,814 --> 00:37:32,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're an Arahant, that's it.

492
00:37:33,356 --> 00:37:35,858
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter... and there was... he was...

493
00:37:36,139 --> 00:37:39,361
[SPEAKER_00]: I think he was slightly mocking them, but he was also proving a point.

494
00:37:40,242 --> 00:37:45,286
[SPEAKER_00]: There was that divine... he was... he would do this divine... divinations, yeah.

495
00:37:45,726 --> 00:37:46,767
[SPEAKER_00]: He would look at the skull.

496
00:37:48,228 --> 00:37:54,714
[SPEAKER_00]: They would give him a skull of a dead person and he would tap on it and do some things and they say, he is reborn in such and such place.

497
00:37:56,342 --> 00:38:02,770
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think there must have been something to... he might have had some of the abilities to see the future rebirth, which doesn't... means nothing.

498
00:38:03,210 --> 00:38:05,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Butujana can see future rebirths.

499
00:38:06,675 --> 00:38:07,596
[SPEAKER_00]: It's no salvation.

500
00:38:07,676 --> 00:38:10,620
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... if I tell you tomorrow, you'll most likely be home.

501
00:38:11,820 --> 00:38:16,122
[SPEAKER_00]: In ten years, you might be... next life, you might be some... like at such-and-such place.

502
00:38:16,682 --> 00:38:18,563
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's within the same nature of things.

503
00:38:18,583 --> 00:38:23,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I tell you in five minutes you'll do this, and in, you know, five hundred lifetimes you'll do that.

504
00:38:23,964 --> 00:38:26,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a greater on a scale, but it's the same thing.

505
00:38:27,026 --> 00:38:38,670
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when they brought this skull to this man of... of a monk who died, who was an arahant, he tapped it, and then he was confused, and then he... he kept tapping it, and he couldn't... he couldn't see anything.

506
00:38:39,199 --> 00:38:41,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, well, that's because he's not reborn anyway.

507
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:46,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's exactly that.

508
00:38:47,421 --> 00:38:50,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, the thing is, but the problem is people don't take it like that.

509
00:38:50,842 --> 00:38:56,143
[SPEAKER_00]: They take it as a setting stone divine, like completely blessed.

510
00:38:56,263 --> 00:38:58,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm blessed because I was told.

511
00:38:58,664 --> 00:38:58,864
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

512
00:38:59,684 --> 00:39:02,325
[SPEAKER_00]: At the time when you were told, if that person who told you

513
00:39:03,195 --> 00:39:04,056
[SPEAKER_00]: You could truly see it.

514
00:39:04,936 --> 00:39:07,578
[SPEAKER_00]: You were heading at the heavenly destination.

515
00:39:08,279 --> 00:39:11,301
[SPEAKER_00]: That's because back then, your behavior was good.

516
00:39:12,062 --> 00:39:12,802
[SPEAKER_00]: But they don't tell you that.

517
00:39:12,822 --> 00:39:14,804
[SPEAKER_00]: They just tell you, you go to heaven and that's it.

518
00:39:15,104 --> 00:39:18,647
[SPEAKER_00]: I spend the rest of my life thinking, I go to heaven no matter what I do.

519
00:39:19,187 --> 00:39:25,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what Christians, what they've been doing, you know, the Crusaders' wars and stuff.

520
00:39:25,732 --> 00:39:30,896
[SPEAKER_00]: You go plundering, raping, murdering, stealing, with the belief of you going to heaven.

521
00:39:32,057 --> 00:39:32,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you were told.

522
00:39:33,891 --> 00:39:37,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, even that depends on your right now actions.

523
00:39:38,373 --> 00:39:49,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So you might... you will be better off if somebody tells you, if you behave wholesomely, if you don't give in to anger, don't give in to greed, don't give in to any aversion, you'll most likely go to heaven.

524
00:39:50,557 --> 00:39:53,078
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need any special natural powers to know that.

525
00:39:53,118 --> 00:39:56,500
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need a simple intelligent reflection, which I started this talk with.

526
00:39:57,340 --> 00:40:02,282
[SPEAKER_00]: When somebody thinks, well, whether I misbehave or not, if there's a future life or not, it's better to behave.

527
00:40:03,301 --> 00:40:11,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You're better off if somebody tells you that, because then, for the rest of your life, you'll be doing things rightly, with that thought of a good rebirth.

528
00:40:11,167 --> 00:40:12,668
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha did say that, actually.

529
00:40:12,828 --> 00:40:19,894
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, behaving properly, keeping the sīla and virtues, goes to more fortunate destinations.

530
00:40:21,074 --> 00:40:25,498
[SPEAKER_00]: That's better to be told than if somebody just tells you, oh, yes, you're heading to heaven, and you take it for granted.

531
00:40:27,070 --> 00:40:31,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they truly knew that you were heading to heaven, they probably wouldn't just tell you you're heading to heaven.

532
00:40:31,813 --> 00:40:35,796
[SPEAKER_00]: They would tell you you're heading to heaven if you keep behaving properly like you're doing now.

533
00:40:37,497 --> 00:40:41,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... unless you hear that, then be a bit more doubtful, just in case.

534
00:40:42,461 --> 00:40:47,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, even if they're right or not, you can't tell, so it's better to be on the safe side.

535
00:40:48,205 --> 00:40:49,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how the Buddha approached it.

536
00:40:49,525 --> 00:40:56,010
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean there's absolutely nothing to it, but whatever it is, it still depends on what you do right here, right now.

537
00:40:56,758 --> 00:40:57,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't really mean much.

